WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
Imagine you're standing on a crowded city bus, the doors have just closed, the engine

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.760
groans, and suddenly your heart starts pounding against your ribs, your palms are sweating,

3
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.600
you feel this wave of absolute suffocating terror.

4
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.400
Oh, that's a nightmare scenario.

5
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.400
Right.

6
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.520
Now, most people watching you grip the handrail with white knuckles would guess they'd think

7
00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.240
you have social anxiety.

8
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.960
But what if the reason you're panicking has absolutely nothing to do with the people

9
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:28.960
around you?

10
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.440
What's the anxiety you're feeling is something structurally, fundamentally different.

11
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:36.320
I mean, it's a completely different mechanism at play.

12
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.240
But from the outside, the behavior, you know, the panic, the avoidance, it looks identical.

13
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.240
Totally identical.

14
00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:44.240
Right.

15
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.680
And that visual overlap is exactly where so many of us, including professionals, misread

16
00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.360
what is actually happening in the brain.

17
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:55.000
Okay, let's unpack this because today we're cracking open the secret language that clinicians

18
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.040
use to decode human behavior.

19
00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.040
I love that.

20
00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:59.320
It's a secret language.

21
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:00.640
It really is.

22
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.720
We got our hands on some incredibly detailed prep course notes for this deep dive.

23
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:09.560
They're from an instructor named Keek over at Pass Ready Prep.

24
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:13.600
And these notes are meant for people taking clinical licensing exams, focusing on the

25
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:19.480
smaller sort of glossed over categories of the anxiety family, the diagnostic F codes.

26
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.040
Which I love framing as a secret language, honestly, because when people say, you know,

27
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.360
I'm anxious, it's this giant gray catch all umbrella.

28
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:27.360
Oh, absolutely.

29
00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.600
But in the clinical manual, anxiety isn't just one flavor.

30
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.400
It's highly specific.

31
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.160
And these particular codes, these smaller categories, they're precisely the areas where the most

32
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.680
massive diagnostic blind spots occur.

33
00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.400
Well let's start right in the F40.2 range, specific phobia.

34
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:47.880
The notes define this as a marked out of proportion fear of one particular object or

35
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:48.880
situation.

36
00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.200
So like heights, needles, dogs, flying.

37
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.200
Yeah.

38
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.280
But the threshold here isn't just, I really hate spiders, right?

39
00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.280
Far from it.

40
00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.040
If you've actually hit the F40.2 criteria, this fear has to last at least six months

41
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.880
and it has to involve active life altering avoidance.

42
00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:04.880
Life altering?

43
00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:05.880
Yes.

44
00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.280
We are talking about someone who will take a three day train ride across the country

45
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.440
just to avoid a two hour flight, even if it means losing a promotion at work or someone

46
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.160
who hasn't gone into their own basement in eight months because they saw a spider down

47
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:20.480
there once.

48
00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.240
The avoidance literally dictates the architecture of their daily life.

49
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:31.560
Which brings us to the first massive misconception or I guess clinical pitfall in the source

50
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.080
material.

51
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.240
It's about how you treat that level of avoidance.

52
00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.440
Because if someone is ruining their career to avoid a plane, the instinct is get this

53
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.520
person a prescription fast.

54
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:43.920
Which is completely wrong.

55
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:44.920
Wait, really?

56
00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:45.920
Totally wrong.

57
00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.160
The absolute gold standard, the first line treatment for a specific phobia is exposure

58
00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.040
therapy, not medication.

59
00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.880
If you're looking at a treatment plan for F40.2 and it leads with prescribing a pill, it's

60
00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:57.880
a major misstep.

61
00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.040
I want to understand the mechanics of why that is.

62
00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.280
Why does a pill fail here?

63
00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.320
Because a specific phobia isn't a generalized chemical imbalance that you can just dial

64
00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.680
down with a sedative.

65
00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:10.300
It is a highly localized learned association.

66
00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:16.240
The brain's amygdala has incorrectly tagged one specific input, say an airplane, as a

67
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:17.920
legal existential threat.

68
00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:18.920
Right.

69
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:22.700
So a pill might make you drowsy or maybe chemically numb your nervous system

70
00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.840
temporarily, but it doesn't untag the threat.

71
00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.200
The moment the medication wears off, the brain still believes airplanes equal death.

72
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.240
So it's almost like a, wait, let me try this.

73
00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:35.560
It's like a home security system that works perfectly 99% of the time.

74
00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:36.560
Okay, I'm with you.

75
00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.040
But the owner accidentally programmed it to trigger the most massive ear splitting police

76
00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.640
dispatching alarm only when the neighbor's golden retriever walks by.

77
00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:46.640
Yes.

78
00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.440
When you unplug the alarm speaker, you have to actually reprogram the sensor.

79
00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.120
That is a great way to look at it.

80
00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.740
And the reprogramming is exposure therapy.

81
00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.480
The only way to rewire that specific neural pathway is habituation.

82
00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.320
So you have to trigger the alarm system on purpose.

83
00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.480
Exactly.

84
00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.820
You have the person look at a picture of a dog, then a video.

85
00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.960
Then they stand 20 feet from a dog.

86
00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.960
They sit with the noise of that alarm, feeling the panic, until the brain's fear

87
00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:15.640
center eventually realizes, oh, the house didn't burn down.

88
00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.320
We survived.

89
00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.360
You proved the threat is false through lived experience.

90
00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:24.160
You let the dog walk past the sensor until the system stops caring?

91
00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.000
That makes total mechanical sense.

92
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.000
Yeah, it really does.

93
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.160
But what happens when that security sensor isn't triggered by a specific object,

94
00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.160
like a dog or a plane, but by the physical environment itself?

95
00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.480
Like what if the terror is about the feeling that you absolutely cannot escape the room?

96
00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:47.880
Then we leave F40.2 and enter agoraphobia, which is coded as F40.00.

97
00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.840
The defining feature shifts here.

98
00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:54.960
It is the fear of two or more situations where escape might be difficult or where help might

99
00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.340
be unavailable if you start to panic.

100
00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:02.260
And the source notes list things like public transit, open parking lots, enclosed spaces

101
00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:07.040
like movie theaters, standing in crowds or lines, or just being outside of the home alone.

102
00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.440
Notice how broad that is compared to a specific phobia.

103
00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.940
Yeah, it's everywhere.

104
00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:15.080
Because the anxiety is bleeding into multiple environments, the treatment approach has to

105
00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:16.800
shift.

106
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.160
Exposure therapy is still a core component, usually wrapped into cognitive behavioral therapy

107
00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:22.160
or CBT.

108
00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:23.160
Right.

109
00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.280
But here, it is frequently paired with an SSRI medication.

110
00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.640
Wait, let me pause you there.

111
00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:32.040
We throw around the term SSRI a lot, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor.

112
00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.040
Right.

113
00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.000
But for those of us who aren't writing prescriptions, what exactly is that doing in the brain

114
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.960
to help the CBT work better?

115
00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:44.000
Like, why is a pill okay here, but not for the specific phobia?

116
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.620
It's a great distinction.

117
00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.480
Serotonin is a neurotransmitter that helps regulate mood and anxiety.

118
00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:53.960
An SSRI blocks the brain from reabsorbing serotonin too quickly.

119
00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.840
So it leaves more of an active in the gap between your neurons.

120
00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:57.840
Precisely.

121
00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.480
For someone with agoraphobia, their baseline level of panic is often simmering so high

122
00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:05.480
across so many different daily environments that they are too overwhelmed to even attempt

123
00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:06.480
therapy.

124
00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.680
So the SSRI acts as a chemical buffer.

125
00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.880
It lowers the floor of that ambient panic just enough so the patient can actually engage

126
00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.560
with the CBT and do the hard work of exposure.

127
00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.680
Okay, that clarifies the treatment.

128
00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:23.960
But going back to the diagnosis, let's return to that crowded city bus scenario we started

129
00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:28.640
with because Keek's notes highlight a massive blind spot here.

130
00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.120
Yeah, they do.

131
00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:34.440
From the outside, agoraphobia looks identical to social anxiety.

132
00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.240
What's fascinating here is that this is the most common mix-up in the book.

133
00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:44.200
If you see someone refusing to go to a concert or sweating on a crowded bus, the default assumption

134
00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.320
is usually social anxiety.

135
00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:47.720
Because there are people around.

136
00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:48.720
Right.

137
00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.600
But the internal mechanism, the actual why, is completely different.

138
00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:54.760
Social anxiety is fundamentally about scrutiny.

139
00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:00.520
It is the deep fear of being judged, humiliated, or negatively evaluated by others.

140
00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:05.480
Agoraphobia, on the other hand, is strictly about being trapped or stranded without help.

141
00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:07.280
So it's escape versus scrutiny.

142
00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:08.280
Precisely.

143
00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:12.000
If we break down the mechanics of the therapy, how does that shift the actual work you do

144
00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.000
with a patient?

145
00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:16.780
Like, if I'm terrified of the bus and you misdiagnose me with social anxiety, what happens?

146
00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:18.560
We waste months of time.

147
00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.080
Yeah, because if I think you have social anxiety, my CBT work with you is going to

148
00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:26.000
focus on cognitive restructuring around judgment.

149
00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:30.640
I'll ask you, what's the worst that happens if someone sees you sweating?

150
00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:32.400
Are they really staring at your shoes?

151
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.200
You're reality testing the social evaluation.

152
00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:36.200
Exactly.

153
00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:42.400
But if you actually have F40000 agoraphobia, you don't care if people are staring at

154
00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:43.400
your shoes.

155
00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:46.200
You are terrified that the bus doors are going to jam, you're going to have a heart

156
00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:49.000
attack, and the paramedics won't be able to reach you.

157
00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.000
Oh, wow.

158
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.240
So talking about self-esteem is useless.

159
00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:53.240
Completely useless.

160
00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:57.160
Agoraphobia, the therapy has to focus on inter-receptive exposure.

161
00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:58.160
Which is what?

162
00:07:58.160 --> 00:07:59.160
Exactly.

163
00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:02.160
Getting you comfortable with the physical sensation of a racing heart, building your

164
00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.440
confidence that you can survive a panic symptom without needing an immediate escape

165
00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:07.440
hatch.

166
00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:10.500
You really have to know what's actually broken before you try to fix it.

167
00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:14.840
So we've established that agoraphobia is essentially a terror of being detached from

168
00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:18.320
a safe escape route or access to medical help.

169
00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:24.040
But what if that vital sense of safety isn't tied to a door or a hospital or a

170
00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:25.360
location at all?

171
00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.520
What if your entire sense of safety is tethered to a person?

172
00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:34.240
That transitions us perfectly to the third clinical category in the notes.

173
00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:36.840
Separation anxiety disorder, F93.0.

174
00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:37.840
Okay.

175
00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.160
This is defined as a developmentally inappropriate excessive fear about being

176
00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:45.240
separated from specific attached figures.

177
00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:50.400
The clinical manifestations are intense, immense distress at separation, persistent

178
00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.240
nightmares about losing the person, constant worry that they will come to harm.

179
00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.680
And a total refusal to sleep away from them, right?

180
00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:57.680
Exactly.

181
00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:01.360
And the standard treatment is CBT, often family-based, because the anxiety is so

182
00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:03.080
intertwined with the family dynamic.

183
00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:05.920
But here's where it gets really interesting.

184
00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:10.040
There is a massive diagnostic update here that blows up a lot of assumptions.

185
00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:11.040
Huge update.

186
00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.360
Separation anxiety disorder is no longer just a childhood diagnosis.

187
00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:18.080
The manual explicitly recognizes it in fully grown adults.

188
00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:21.240
Which represents a monumental paradigm shift in psychology.

189
00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:24.720
Historically, the field viewed this purely as a pediatric issue.

190
00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.040
Like you just outgrow it.

191
00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:27.040
Right.

192
00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:30.760
If a 30-year-old had extreme anxiety about being away from their spouse, they were slapped

193
00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:37.000
with a label of codependency or borderline personality traits or simply generalized anxiety.

194
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:42.640
But we now recognize that the exact same neurological mechanisms of separation terror

195
00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:43.640
can exist in an adult.

196
00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:44.920
Hold on.

197
00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:45.920
I'm struggling with this one a bit.

198
00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:46.920
I need to push back.

199
00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:51.720
When I hear separation anxiety, I picture a toddler screaming at daycare, you know, wrapping

200
00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:53.840
themselves around their dad's leg.

201
00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:59.120
But an adult, if a 40-year-old is tracking their spouse's phone 10 times a day or refusing

202
00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:05.320
to let them travel for work without melting down, how is that not just toxic control

203
00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:06.320
or extreme jealousy?

204
00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:07.600
It's a fair question.

205
00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:13.120
Where is the line between an F93.0 panic disorder and just being a terribly controlling

206
00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:14.120
learner?

207
00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:17.240
It all comes down to the core motivation, the internal narrative.

208
00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:21.320
Someone with severe jealousy or control issues is tracking the phone because they suspect

209
00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:25.320
infidelity or because they demand dominance over their partner's time.

210
00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:27.160
They're angry or suspicious.

211
00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:32.160
An adult with separation anxiety disorder is tracking the phone out of paralyzing catastrophic

212
00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:33.160
dread.

213
00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:36.800
They aren't checking to see if their spouse is at a bar with someone else.

214
00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.400
They're checking to ensure their spouse isn't dead in a ditch.

215
00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:41.400
Oh.

216
00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:42.480
Oh, wow.

217
00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:45.120
That is a radically different internal experience.

218
00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:46.800
It's pure terror.

219
00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.360
It's the adult who physically cannot sleep a single wink if their partner is out of town

220
00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:56.000
because their brain safety mechanism is entirely outsourced to the presence of that specific

221
00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:57.000
human being.

222
00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:01.280
So when that person leaves, their nervous system registers it as an existential threat.

223
00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:06.760
If we brush that off as just being a little clingy or we judge them as toxic and controlling,

224
00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:09.680
we deny them the specific interventions that can actually help.

225
00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:12.000
How do you even begin to treat that in an adult?

226
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.320
You can't exactly do pediatric family therapy.

227
00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:15.320
No.

228
00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:17.940
You use CBT to help them build internal safety.

229
00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:22.200
You work with them and often the spouse to slowly habituate to distance.

230
00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.200
Like exposure therapy again.

231
00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:25.200
Exactly.

232
00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:29.000
Maybe the spouse goes to the grocery store for 20 minutes and the patient practices

233
00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:32.320
sitting with the discomfort without texting them.

234
00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:34.440
You challenge the catastrophic thoughts.

235
00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:38.020
Just because they haven't replied in 10 minutes doesn't mean they're in a car crash.

236
00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:41.000
You rebuild their tolerance for independence.

237
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:45.360
That forces such a profound reevaluation of adult relationships.

238
00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:50.360
We are so quick to judge behavior on the surface without asking what the underlying architecture

239
00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:51.760
of that fear actually is.

240
00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:52.840
We really are.

241
00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:57.040
Which really sets the stage for the final category in Keek's Prep Notes.

242
00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:00.400
We've talked about the panic of being separated from a safe person.

243
00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:05.320
Now let's look at how anxiety can completely alter our physical ability to interact when

244
00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:09.720
we are thrust into expected social situations without that safety net.

245
00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:14.160
We are talking about selective mutism, code F94.0A.

246
00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:19.500
The diagnostic criteria require consistent failure to speak in specific social situations

247
00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:23.360
where speaking is expected despite speaking comfortably in other situations.

248
00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:27.420
The classic hallmark being the child who is a total chatterbox at home but absolutely

